## Phase D: logger specification and uncertainty (complete)

**What must a cheap, battery-powered box measure so that nothing important is missed?**
Phase D uses the response results to define the logger, compares ways of storing data, and shows how uncertain the damage estimate is.

### Logger requirements
| Requirement | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Measured quantity | 2 horizontal accelerations at the highest accessible point, plus inclination and temperature | The top moves the most |
| Range | +/- 1 g | Covers storms and a strong shaking event (about 0.44 g at the damage limit for the tower stage) |
| Noise floor | at most about 29 ug/sqrt(Hz) from 0.03 to 2 Hz | The smallest sea state that still matters gives only about 0.4 mg of motion on the bare pile |
| Frequency band | 0.03 to 7 Hz | From the wave band up to the second mode of the bare pile |
| Sampling rate | 25 Hz for raw bursts, 10 Hz for the always-on channel | At least 2.5 times the highest frequency of interest |

### Logging strategies compared (6 months)
| Strategy | Memory | Power | Keeps | Loses |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Raw continuous | about 2.4 GB | 6.2 mW | Everything | Nothing, but huge memory |
| 10-minute statistics + spectrum | about 3 MB | 4.2 mW | RMS, maximum, spectrum | Cycle order, short events |
| On-board rainflow histogram | about 4 MB | 5.1 mW | Damage directly | Raw waveform |
| Event-triggered bursts + statistics | about 300 to 700 MB | 4.6 to 5.2 mW | Full waveforms where they matter | Data outside the triggers |

Memory differs by a factor of about 1000, but energy hardly differs (about 18 to 27 Wh), because the always-on sensor dominates. One primary lithium cell is enough for 6 months in this model.

**Recommendation:** a hybrid. Always-on 10-minute statistics with a spectrum, an hourly rainflow histogram, and raw bursts when the wind enters the risky band or a storm arrives. The first thing to measure is the shaking amplitude of the tower stage, because this study cannot close that unknown.

### Uncertainty (Monte Carlo, 1500 samples)
The study varies soil stiffness, damping, wave severity, the VIV amplitude and how often lock-in actually occurs.

| Result | Value |
|---|---|
| Bare monopile, waves | median 0.00035, 95th percentile 0.0027, 99th percentile 0.0065 |
| Tower stage, waves + VIV scenario | median 0.00075, 95th percentile 0.008 |
| Chance of exceeding the allowable damage (tower stage) | about 0.3 % |
| What drives the spread | wave severity first, then VIV amplitude and damping. Soil stiffness hardly matters for the bare stages |

![Monte Carlo spread](fig_mc.png)
![What the logger must resolve](fig_logger.png)

### Limits
- Logger hardware numbers (sensor 3 mW, microcontroller 30 mW active, 1 uJ per stored byte) are assumptions. Check them against data sheets.
- The Monte Carlo input distributions are judgement, not measured data.
- Signal levels come from the first mode only, so high-frequency content is a lower bound.

Script: `phase_d_logger_mc.py`.
